{"kind":"report","text":"Ruling: published anchor lists in archive passes (answers request 26e4c886; carries decision 5a642337 into the archive format).\n\nDecision 5a642337 (item 3) made the anchor list part of the export. Slice 9 computes it (planned.anchors) and the driver drops it, so a clone cannot read it. Your diagnosis is right. The rulings:\n\n1. A field, not a file. Each v2 pass manifest (pass.json) gets an optional field `anchors`: the list that the planner produced for that pass, exactly as planArchive returns it. It is in the same pass commit, so the pass's integrity covers it. A field adds no tree node, so the node budget of decision f651f6c3 is unchanged; its bytes count in the pass metadata budget. Readers check its form: each anchor names a register or membership fact by scope, incarnation, position and hash; the list is sorted by scope and position, has no duplicates, names no fact of an archived journal, and has at most as many items as the pass's admitted copies.\n\n2. Caller's word. The CLI lists every anchor as taken on the caller's word, as the replay report does now (report.ts:39-40). Offline replay uses a published list only with an explicit trust option. It never trusts one by default.\n\n3. Legacy passes. A pass with no `anchors` field reports the category `anchors-unpublished`, names the pass, and refuses full operator acceptance. A reader never derives a substitute list for it and never says that one was published.\n\n4. Passes already deployed. Yes, they need an explicit export. The first pass that the new code writes for a journal also writes `legacyAnchors`: the anchors that every earlier pass of that journal needs, computed by the same planner function from the entries that the scope still holds, with the range of positions it covers. The commit says what it is: anchors published at this pass for earlier passes, not with them. A reader treats an earlier pass as covered from that commit on.\n\n5. Order against F3. The earlier entries must still be held when legacyAnchors is computed. So F3 enables no floor on a journal until every one of its passes is covered by a published list. A floor entry that would delete entries of an uncovered pass is refused. State the rule in the F3 head and add a witness.\n\n6. Witnesses for 7b19837c: a new pass publishes its anchors and a clone replays with the trust option; the same clone without the option stops at the first anchored fact; a legacy pass reports anchors-unpublished; after the first new pass, legacyAnchors covers the legacy passes and full acceptance passes; a manifest with an anchor for an archived journal, a duplicate or an unsorted list is refused by the reader.\n\n7. Unchanged: no anchor is invented or trusted by default, the register and membership journals stay unexported, and the hosted register remains how a caller confirms the directory genesis source (register seq 2, sha256:8682b3bb…).","body":{"to":"builder"}}